# Tags are for the reader

The site is called Notes. Every writing is already a note. Tags exist so a **reader** can browse, navigate, map ideas, and follow connections between pieces.

They do not exist to describe your process, to feed `/tag` prelude code, to pad SEO, or to make Writing Insights charts happier.

## Do

- Tag **what the piece is about** and **which ideas it connects to** (`fatherhood`, `puerto-rico`, `boundaries`, `cooking`).
- Prefer tags a stranger would click to find more of that idea.
- Keep distinct ideas distinct (`parenting` vs `fatherhood`; English `limits` vs `boundaries`).
- One becoming door per piece (`personal-growth` or `transformation` or `self-improvement`), not the hallway stacked.
- One inner-work door per piece (`consciousness` or `self-reflection` or `mindfulness` or `inner-work`), not the hallway stacked.
- One heal door per piece (`healing` or `therapy` or `mental-health`), not the hallway stacked.
- Form tags (`poems`, `memoir`, `reflection`) only when a reader would browse that shelf.

## Do not

- Never tag `notes`, `note`, `nota`, or `notas`. Canonicalize strips them; `/tag/notes` 301s to `/tag`.
- Do not add `essays`, `ideas`, or other empty form labels just because a spec wants a prelude inventory.
- Do not invent tags for Maslow buckets, tag weights, or schema.

The `/tag` index is an idea map, not an author inventory. Do not restore “I've written #essays, #notes…” copy.

Full policy: `docs/tag-policy.md`. Vocabulary: `src/data/tagVocabulary.ts`.
